{
  "title": "Use blocking priority passes in the Windows subscription player",
  "description": "Offline audit of one completed Commander duel on f1ecd8ae found 98 MCP calls: 93 choose_action and only two pass_priority. 57 recorded choose_action(choice=no) calls followed GAME_SELECT boolean prompts with no listed choices. GUI prompt advice alone did not produce efficient passing. Investigate enforcing the intended passing contract in the GUI adapter/tool policy, using native legal-action detection. Empty choices alone must not authorize arbitrary boolean answers. The existing native pass operation deliberately passes its first current priority window; later windows stop for playable non-mana actions, combat and non-priority decisions. Never blanket-yield until my_turn. Acceptance: deterministic regressions for legal instants/abilities, combat, mulligan, targets, triggers, cancellation/elimination/end, stale state and prolonged human waits; preserve model/effort and private-information boundaries; compare live token/call metrics only after offline validation. No claim that 57 calls translates to a fixed quota saving. See doc/windows-subscription-usage-audit.md.",
